WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.050
Lego is one of the most popular toys ever,
so it's not a huge shock that The Lego Movie

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:07.869
was a huge hit when it was released in 2014.

00:00:07.869 --> 00:00:12.070
A little surprising, however, was just how
good it actually was.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.460
It's the rare kids' movie that adults can
truly enjoy, full of subtle jokes and references

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.100
targeted specifically at older viewers.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.190
Here are a few things only the grown-ups in
the theater noticed.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.190
Legal woes

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:29.530
A big part of what makes The Lego Movie so
fun is its authentic eye toward how real kids

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:30.760
play with Lego.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.090
Most kids probably don't keep all their different
types of Lego separate, and The Lego Movie

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.730
fittingly brings together a mixed set of characters,
many of them licensed.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:44.890
"Please, calm yourselves, Green Ninja, Milhouse,
Nice Vampire, Michelangelo, Michelangelo,

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.190
and Cleopatra."

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.340
Kids don't know or c are much about intellectual
property rights and copyright law, but most

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.310
adults watching with a critical eye knew what
a legal nightmare it must have been to get

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:59.050
the rights to use so many different pop cultural
entities in one place.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:03.460
Co-directors and writers Phil Lord and Christopher
Miller have since confirmed it was a logistical

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:09.450
nightmare, and with every major script revision,
rights-holders had to be approached anew.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.670
Clever casting

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.749
Savvy adults with a taste for quirky comedies
surely spotted a few clever voice casting

00:01:15.749 --> 00:01:17.579
decisions in The Lego Movie.

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:22.850
For example, co-directors Lord and Miller
also created MTV's early 2000s cult classic

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:28.359
cartoon Clone High, about the teenage clones
of a number of historical figures.

00:01:28.359 --> 00:01:33.829
Will Forte voiced teen Lincoln in Clone High,
and reprises his role as Lego Lincoln.

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:38.180
Channing Tatum and Jonah Hill play Lego Superman
and Lego Green Lantern, respectively …

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.600
"I can't move."

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.340
"Don't worry, Superman.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.300
I'll get you out of there!"

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:42.840
"No, don't!"

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.060
"Ah!

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.520
My hands are stuck!

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.049
My legs are stuck, as well."

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:48.049
"I super-hate you."

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:53.270
… and they had previously teamed up in the
big-screen adaptation of 21 Jump Street, directed

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.189
by Lord and Miller.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:56.189
Books and bricks

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:00.950
There are some relatively obscure literary
and historical references scattered throughout

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.130
The Lego Movie.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:06.609
The fantasy realm is named "Middle Zealand,"
a combination of Middle Earth, the setting

00:02:06.609 --> 00:02:11.650
of The Lord of the Rings books, and New Zealand,
where Peter Jackson's Lord of the Rings movies

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.650
were filmed.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:18.000
Meanwhile, Cloud Cuckoo Land, Princess Unikitty's
bright and shiny homeland in the sky, is a

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.980
direct literary reference.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.290
The idea of an impossibly perfect city in
the clouds of the same name first shows up

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:30.629
in all the way back in 414 B.C. in The Birds,
a play written by the Greek playwright Aristophanes.

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:35.620
And then there's the wise sage Vitruvius:
He's named after a 1st century B.C. Roman

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.569
philosopher best known for writing a 10-volume
series of books about architecture — fitting

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:43.129
for a character in a flick made of bricks.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.849
Honey, How'd I Meet Your Mother?

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:51.099
The most popular TV show in the Lego-verse
is a dumb, one-joke affair called Where Are

00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:56.580
My Pants?, where a pants-less character frequently
wonders aloud about the whereabouts of his

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.580
pants.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:01.430
"Honey, where are my pants?"

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.379
Kids may miss it, but adults recognize it's
a jab at sitcoms that still air long after

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:09.200
their once-fresh premises have grown stale.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.170
Sitcoms like, say, How I Met Your Mother,
which took eight seasons to introduce the

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.840
audience to the namesake mother … and also
happened to feature The Lego Movie's Phil

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.800
Lord and Christopher Miller as writers during
its first season.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.219
Inside jokes

00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:28.019
There's a scene where Wyldstyle names a bunch
of the realms of the Lego universe, and each

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:29.439
gets a second or two on screen …

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:32.989
"Your home, Bricksburg, is one of many realms
in the universe.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:36.220
There’s also this one, Pirates’ Cove,
Knights’ Club, Vikings Landing, Clown Town,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.459
a bunch of others we don’t need to mention."

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:43.810
Savvy older viewers know why: because they're
discontinued or less popular Lego sets, such

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.170
as the controversial "for girls" Friends line,
a set based on the big-screen bomb of Speed

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:55.170
Racer, the sci-fi Bionicle line, which disappeared
from stores in 2010, and the despised Fabuland

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.400
line from the 1980s.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.920
Mixed signals

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.469
It's not every day that a cheery comedy movie
made for kids is also a subversive satire.

00:04:04.469 --> 00:04:09.889
A critical read of The Lego Movie shows that
it's quite anti-corporate and anti-capitalist.

00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:16.310
The villain of the movie is an evil, money-and-power
hungry tycoon and tyrant named President Business,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.660
known later as Lord Business.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:23.200
Shortly after The Lego Movie's release in
2014, figures from the political left, such

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.161
as documentary filmmaker Michael Moore, praised
the film's anti-business sentiment, while

00:04:28.161 --> 00:04:32.440
more conservative entities, such as the Fox
Business Channel, decried it.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.800
"Y'know, listen, Hollywood has its own agenda,
and we're kinda used to this, but it feels,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.730
it feels a little bit more threatening when
they start to push this out to our kids."

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:45.600
Of course, can a movie that also serves as
a highly effective feature-length commercial

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.050
for a toy line really be that anti-capitalism?

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.200
Pure imagination

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.340
At the end of the movie, it's revealed that
the action up to that point took place within

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:00.150
the imagination of a Lego-loving little boy
playing with a massive Lego collection owned

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.880
by his father, played by Will Ferrell, who
also provides the voice of Lord Business.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.770
So all of that world-building, world-mixing,
and searching for the "piece of resistance"

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.520
didn't actually happen.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.010
It was all just a figment of the kid's imagination,
and a neat little parable about creativity,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.780
trust, and believing in yourself.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.680
But by the time the sequel-teasing ending
rolls around, most kids have already forgotten

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.950
all that, if they got the message in the first
place ...

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:32.030
"We're from the planet Duplo, and we're here
to destroy you."

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.240
"Oh man."

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.550
Thanks for watching!

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:37.060
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.250
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

